1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,500
Welcome back. Today we start Florida wills, and the first thing we need is intestate succession.

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:15,000
When someone dies without a valid will, the intestacy statute decides who the heirs are.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:27,000
If the decedent leaves a surviving spouse and all of the lineal descendants are also descendants of that spouse, the spouse takes everything.

4
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:41,000
But if there are descendants from outside the marriage, the surviving spouse share drops to one half, and the descendants split the rest per stirpes.

5
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Watch out for half blood relatives. In this state a half blood relative takes only half as much as a whole blood relative of the same degree. That shows up on essays.

6
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Remember too the slayer rule, and the simultaneous death rule when nobody can tell who died first.

7
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Now let's switch to protecting the spouse when there is a will. The big one is the elective share.

8
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:48,000
The surviving spouse can elect thirty percent of the elective estate, and the elective estate is much broader than the probate estate.

9
00:01:48,000 --> 00:02:05,000
It pulls in revocable trusts, joint accounts, and pay on death accounts. And the right to elect can be waived in a prenuptial waiver if there was fair disclosure.

10
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:25,000
Then there is the pretermitted spouse. If you marry after making the will and the will never provides for you, you take an intestate share unless an exception applies.

11
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:40,000
And finally homestead. The descent of homestead is restricted when there is a spouse or minor child, so a devise of homestead can fail.
